 "name": "Meadow, Ocean or City View: SART Attention Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Johnson, Pontvianne, Ly, Jin, Januar, Machida, Sargent, Lee, Williams & Williams (2022), \"Water and Meadow Views Both Afford Perceived but Not Performance-Based Attention Restoration: Results From Two Experiments\", Frontiers in Psychology 13: 809629 (Study 2, the online experiment). Participants give age, sex, handedness and a Karolinska Sleepiness Scale rating (1 extremely alert … 9 extremely sleepy), do an 18-trial practice and a 225-trial Sustained Attention to Response Task (digits 1–9 in 25 shuffled cycles, no two 3s in a row; 250 ms digit + 900 ms circled-cross mask; press the space bar or tap for every digit except 3), look at one photograph for 40 s full screen (meadow, ocean or ground-level urban street, assigned at random), repeat the 225-trial SART, rate sleepiness again and rate the view's restorativeness. The page stores omission and commission errors, mean and SD of reaction time and a trial log for each SART (keyed \"baseline\" / \"post\"). Original result (N = 186 students and adults, Melbourne): meadow and ocean views were rated more restorative than the urban view (PRS means 3.8 and 4.1 vs 2.8 on 0–6) and did not differ from each other, but SART performance after the view did not differ between groups. Photographs: Figures 1, 2 and 4 of the paper (CC BY 4.0). Differences from the original study: (1) the SART runs in the browser instead of Inquisit; (2) perceived restorativeness is measured with four items written for this template (being away, fascination, coherence, compatibility, 0–6) instead of the 26-item PRS, and the photograph is shown again next to them; (3) the Attention-Related Cognitive Errors Scale and the Connectedness to Nature Scale are omitted; (4) KSS shows only its end labels; (5) participants are not screened for diagnoses or medication on the page.",
